// The platform-free half of the parking port (runtime/park/park.bit, #1403),
// exercised as a real linked binary on every target.
//
// `runtime/park` splits the way `runtime/alloc` and `runtime/rand` do: the core
// holds the policy and the wait CONTRACT, and the two providers hold the
// platform calls. This program covers the core; tests/stress/parklinux and
// tests/stress/parkdarwin cover the providers, and all three share
// ./props/props.bit.
//
// The backoff schedule is the whole of the scheduler's current idle policy —
// `sched.zig`'s `Worker.run` does `sleepNs(backoff)` then
// `backoff = @min(backoff * 2, max_backoff_ns)`, seeded at `min_backoff_ns` —
// so it is checked as an exact KAT rather than a range. A drift in this
// schedule is not a crash: it is an idle worker that either burns CPU (too
// short) or reacts to new work too late (too long), and neither shows up as a
// wrong answer anywhere else in the suite.
//
// Two values, both deterministic on every target: the backoff KAT, and the
// constants themselves. The constants are printed rather than merely compared
// so that a change to `sched.zig`'s window shows up here as a visible diff
// against the .expected file, forcing the two to be reconciled deliberately.

import { parkMinBackoffNs, parkMaxBackoffNs } from "../../../runtime/park"
import { parkBackoffKat } from "./props"

function main() {
  print("${parkBackoffKat()} ${parkMinBackoffNs} ${parkMaxBackoffNs}\n")
}
